  "body": "Repeated compilation dominates this native nine-stage checker fixture: the unchanged GCC build takes seconds while both complete native word checks take milliseconds. This opt-in experiment uses ccache object reuse, fresh linking and fresh native execution, reducing the measured local build-and-replay median from **2.753 s to 0.240 s (11.46\u00d7)**. A fresh cache fill takes **2.942 s**; this is a repeat-build benefit, not a whole selected-replay or first-use speedup.\n\nThe source engine is unchanged `research/e8-bit-carrier-skeleton/code/bit/checkers/columns_k.cpp`, pinned with its JSON header and upstream notice. The two exact RAW6 fixtures come from our #2373 at `b52f87f5355ad9f02ca4ac4395117655ac1b19c7` (the #1188 baseline and Sagar Thakrar's #2115 deferred-undo word). Every timed sample runs all 1,327 columns, nine stages, signed-prefix bills and 18 omitted-bridge controls per word. All native receipt fields compare, except elapsed time and the exact input path after binding it to the complete consumed payload hash. Reference and cached object hashes agree within each run.\n\nCcache preprocessor mode uses content-based compiler checks, no sloppiness/remote storage and a fresh toolchain-content profile. Source and transitive-header changes preserving both size and mtime cause misses and mathematical rejection. Compile-option and profile changes cause misses; restored inputs hit; a corrupt private cache entry is rebuilt. A wrong word still fails after a hit. The seven actual controls run through the compiler/cache/executable chain.\n\nValidation performed:\n\n- Three interleaved uncached/warm timing pairs and one separate fill, GCC 15.2 / Boost 1.90 / ccache 4.12.3.\n- Full experiment: 13 builds, 24 native executions (21 positive, three deliberate mathematical rejections), including 384 omitted-bridge controls in successful executions.\n- Registered Python 3.12 replay: independent bitset checks of every dirty column and exact prefix bills, plus nine package control groups.\n- Full optimized Python 3.14 experiment and nine groups from a Git-free read-only 18-file execution snapshot: all normalized native results identical, snapshot unchanged; medians 2.761 s / 0.241 s.\n- Metadata/pins pass. A scoped workflow executes the actual native benchmark; no hosted CI success is claimed.\n\n`BENCHMARK.json` records individual measurements, cache counters, commands and execution hashes. The standard registered command intentionally performs Python/input checks only; README gives the separate full native command. No production entry point, frozen source, selected certificate or kappa changes. Complete PR2373 legality/exporter/5/9/11-stage integration and production end-to-end timing remain open.\n\nOne fresh random draw selected verifier-engineering / replay-runtime / source-bound reuse, refined to this one measured boundary without rerolling. Canonical main remains `0fdd7d82de03e43c9e5a1000dfbcd380d1b687d2`, priority revision 4. Final overlap covered 617 open PRs and 1,140 Discussion records with complete pagination and no warnings. Coordination: #2408.\n\nGareth Davidson (@bitplane), with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance; no subagents or independent human review. Native checker lineage (DaysSky, Dylan Crow and predecessor cohort work), Sagar Thakrar's construction and ccache's existing implementation are credited in NOTICE.md. This is a measured integration experiment, not a new cache algorithm or mathematical construction.\n",
